Let $\mathcal{O}_G$ be the local ring of $G$ at the identity. Since $G$ is a smooth variety over $k$, $\mathcal{O}_G$ is a regular local ring. Let $\mathbf{x}=(x_1,\ldots,x_e)$ be a sequence of local parameters in $\mathcal{O}_G$. By \cite[Thm 30.6(i)]{mat}, the ring $\widehat{\mathcal{O}}_G$ is the power series ring in the variables $\mathbf{x}$. If $F=\widehat{G}$, then $F(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{Y})\in \mathcal{O}_G\llbracket \mathbf{Y}\rrbracket$ (the group action is algebraic!). Hence $\mathcal{O}_G$ is a $G$-subring (after identifying $\mathbf{x}$ with $\mathbf{X}$) of $(k\llbracket \mathbf{X}\rrbracket,\mathbb{D}^F)$. Therefore the field of rational functions $k(G)$ has a natural $G$-derivation on it which we call the \emph{canonical  $G$-derivation} on $k(G)$.

We also get a canonical $G[m]$-derivation on $k(G)$.
